# function user(k, n) { const list = [k]; let made; do { made = list.filter(x => x === k); } while (n); return made; }   sink(user);
# The text of filter asks for the species of its receiver: ArraySpeciesCreate gives whatever object the species constructor makes. The call reaches the row of lists alone, which says that a new list is made:
# in the copy the operation is built as `new Array(0)`, so what the call gives is a list that this code alone has, and the next method of a chain has a callee that is proven.
# The library here is a stand-in.
# LIBRARY: own
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: frame {{*}}callee=f3
# CHECK: Array @Cell = NewArrayLike
# RUN(NEVER): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# NEVER-NOT: ArraySpeciesCreate
# The body of the text itself takes whatever comes.
# RUN(ITSELF): walk f3 mechanisms=all
# ITSELF: = ArraySpeciesCreate

